===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# A cry or [[shout]].
#: {{ux|en|He heard a '''call''' from the other side of the room.}}
# The characteristic [[cry]] of a bird or other animal.
#: {{ux|en|That sound is the distinctive '''call''' of the cuckoo bird.}}
# A [[beckon]]ing or [[summon]]ing.
#: {{ux|en|I had to yield to the '''call''' of the wild.}}
#* {{RQ:Spectator|issue=181|date=27 September 1711|page=440|passage=Dependance is a perpetual '''call''' upon humanity, and a greater incitement to tenderness and pity, than any other motive whatsoever.}}
#* {{RQ:Macaulay History of England|volume=V|chapter=XXIII|page=117|passage=But they had hoped that, when peace had been restored, when no '''call''' of duty required him {{q-q|{{w|William III of England}}}} to cross the sea, he would generally, during the summer and autumn, reside in his fair palaces and parks on the banks of the Thames, [...]}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|magazine=Latina|year=2007|volume=11|page=101|passage=We actually have a '''call''' tomorrow, which is a Sunday, right after my bridal shower. I have to make enchiladas for 10 people!}}
# A telephone [[conversation]]; a [[phone call]].
#: {{ux|en|I received several phone '''calls''' today.}}
#: {{ux|en|I received several '''calls''' today.}}
# An instance of calling someone on the telephone.
#: {{ux|en|I made a '''call''' to Jim, but he didn’t answer.}}
# A short [[visit]], usually for [[social]] purposes.
#: {{ux|en|I paid a '''call''' to a dear friend of mine.}}
#* {{RQ:Cowper Task|pages=13–14|pageref=13|passage=He [...] ſeldom waits, / Dependent on the baker's punctual '''call''', / To hear his creaking panniers at the door, / Angry and ſad and his laſt cruſt conſumed.}}
#* {{RQ:Lindsay Age of Consent|year=1962|page=149|passage=Podson stayed till after five, though he handsomely apologized for outstaying a '''call'''. "The fact is, I never think of the time, when I get talking to a really intelligent woman...'}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} A visit by a ship or boat to a port.
#: {{ux|en|The ship made a '''call''' at Southampton.}}
# A [[decision]] or [[judgement]].
#: {{ux|en|That was a good '''call'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1995|author={{w|Paul Vautin}}|title=Turn It Up!|location=Sydney|publisher=Pan Macmillan Australia |page=2|text=Fair '''call''' actually, because don't the girls blow up when you miss an anniversary?}}
# The right to speak at a given time during a debate or other public event; the [[floor]].
#: {{ux|en|The Prime Minister has the '''call'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|I give the '''call''' to the Manager of Opposition Business.}}
# {{lb|en|finance}} {{ellipsis of|en|call option}}.
# {{lb|en|cricket}} The act of calling to the other batsman.
# {{lb|en|cricket}} The [[state]] of being the batsman whose [[role]] it is to call (depends on where the ball goes.)
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} A work [[shift]] which requires one to be [[available]] when [[requested]], i.e. {{m|en|on call}}.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1978|author=w:Alan E. Nourse|title=The Practice|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Cn9qAAAAMAAJ|publisher=Harper & Row|isbn=9780060131944
|passage=page 48: “Mondays would be great, especially after a weekend of '''call'''.”<br>page 56: “{{...}} I’ve got '''call''' tonight, and all weekend, but I’ll be off tomorrow to help you some.”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=William D. Bailey|title=You Will Never Run out of Jesus|publisher=CrossHouse Publishing,|isbn=978-0-929292-24-3|page=29|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=fb3gfJbhL7UC&pg=PA29
|passage=I took general-surgery '''call''' at Bossier Medical Center and asked special permission to take general-medical '''call''', which was gladly given away by the older staff members: {{...}}. You would be surprised at how many surgical cases came out of medical '''call'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=Jamal M. Bullocks; et al|title=Plastic Surgery Emergencies: Principles and Techniques|publisher=Thieme|isbn=978-1-58890-670-0|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=ywMPk5TV-3AC&pg=PP11|page=ix
|passage=We attempted to include all topics that we ourselves have faced while taking plastic surgery '''call''' at the affiliated hospitals in the Texas Medical Center, one of the largest medical centers in the world, which sees over 100,000 patients per day.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Steven Louis Shelley|title=A Practical Guide to Stage Lighting|year=2009|page=171|passage=The columns in the second rectangle show fewer hours, but part of that is due to the fact that there's a division between a work '''call''' and a show '''call'''.}}
# {{lb|en|computing}} The act of jumping to a subprogram, saving the means to return to the original point.
# A statement of a particular [[state]], or [[rule]], made in many games such as [[bridge]], [[craps]], [[jacks]], and so on.
#: {{ux|en|There was a 20 dollar bet on the table, and my '''call''' was 9.}}
# {{senseid|en|poker}} {{lb|en|poker}} The act of matching a bet made by a player who has previously bet in the same round of betting.
# A note blown on the horn to encourage the dogs in a hunt.
# {{lb|en|nautical}} A whistle or pipe, used by the boatswain and his mate to summon the sailors to duty.
# A pipe or other instrument to call birds or animals by imitating their note or cry. A [[game call]].
# An invitation to take charge of or serve a [[church]] as its [[pastor]].
# {{lb|en|archaic}} Vocation; employment; [[calling]].
# {{lb|en|US|law}} A reference to, or statement of, an object, course, distance, or other matter of description in a survey or grant requiring or calling for a corresponding object, etc., on the land.
# {{lb|en|informal|slang|prostitution}} A meeting with a [[client]] for paid sex; [[hookup]]; [[job]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Lyda Longa|title=Internet hookups mean fewer prostitutes on Daytona’s streets, police say|newspaper=w:The Daytona Beach News-Journal|url=https://www.news-journalonline.com/news/20150220/internet-hookups-mean-fewer-prostitutes-on-daytonas-streets-police-say|location=Daytona Beach, Fla.|date=3 March 2015|passage="They have a little network of women that watch out for each other," Morford said. That means that if one prostitute doesn't come back after going out on a '''call'''&nbsp;– whether it's an Internet prostitute or a streetwalker&nbsp;– and the other women can't get hold of her, they get scared, close up shop and won't work, Morford said.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20200920215810/https://www.news-journalonline.com/news/20150220/internet-hookups-mean-fewer-prostitutes-on-daytonas-streets-police-say|archivedate=20 September 2020}}
# {{lb|en|law}} A lawyer who was [[call to the bar|called to the bar]] (became licensed as a lawyer) in a specified year.
#* {{quote-web|en|title=''Korlyakov v. Riesz,'' 2020 ONSC 6622|author=Master K.E. Jolley|url=https://www.canlii.org/en/on/onsc/doc/2020/2020onsc6622/2020onsc6622.html|work=CanLII|date=28 October 2020|accessdate=19 June 2021|text=The work was done by two lawyers, one a 1983 '''call''' and the other a 2010 '''call'''.}}
# {{lb|en|in negative constructions}} Need; necessity.
#: {{ux|en|There's no '''call''' for that kind of bad language!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1865|author=William Stott Banks|title=Wakefield Words|page=11
|passage=CALL 2 need for. "There worn't noa call for nowt o't'soart."}}
#* {{RQ:Gordon Girl in a Band|page=146|chapter=24|text=If and when they took place, our fights mostly centered around how he treated or spoke to me. In the band’s early days, our first drummer, Richard Edson, was the first to notice the dynamic between us. He would stand up for me, saying things like “Hey, man, there’s no '''call''' to talk to her like that.”}}

