vet.content:
{{also|Appendix:Variations of "vet"}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/vɛt/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛt|s=1}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-vet.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Etymology 1===
{{clipping|en|veterinarian}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|colloquial}} A [[veterinarian]] or [[veterinary surgeon]].
#* {{RQ:Guardian
|date=December 14, 2011
|author=Steven Morris
|title=Devon woman jailed for 168 days for killing kitten in microwave
|url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/world/2011/dec/14/woman-killed-kitten-microwave-jail
|passage=Colin Cameron, a '''vet''' who examined the dead animal, said there was "no doubt the kitten would have suffered unnecessarily" before dying.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261013161452/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2011/dec/14/woman-killed-kitten-microwave-jail|archivedate=13 October 2026}}

=====Derived terms=====
{{col|en|paravet|vet tech}}

=====Translations=====
{{trans-see|veterinarian}}

{{trans-top|short form of veterinary surgeon}}<!--only the short forms -->
* Bulgarian: {{t+|bg|ветеринар|m}}
* French: {{t+|fr|véto|m}}
* Galician: {{t+|gl|veterinario|m}}, {{t+|gl|veterinaria|f}}
* Russian: {{t+|ru|вет|m}}
{{trans-bottom}}

===Etymology 2===
{{clipping|en|veteran}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|colloquial|US}} A [[veteran]] (a former soldier or other member of [[armed forces]]).
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1952|author=w:Ralph Ellison|title=w:Invisible Man |publisher=Penguin Books (2014)|page=73|passage=“A former soldier, sir. A '''vet'''. Theyʼre all '''vets''', a little shellshocked.”}}

=====Usage notes=====
Although ''veteran'' can be used in many contexts such as sports or business to describe someone with many years of experience, ''vet'' is more often used for former military personnel.

=====Derived terms=====
{{col|en|nonvet}}

=====Translations=====
{{trans-see|veteran}}

===Etymology 3===
Possibly by analogy from Etymology 1, in the sense of "verifying the soundness [of an animal]"

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# To thoroughly check or [[investigate]] particularly with regard to providing [[formal]] [[approval]].
#: {{ux|en|The FBI '''vets''' all nominees to the Federal bench.}}
#* {{quote-av|en|date=1980-03-10|writers=w:Antony Jay; w:Jonathan Lynn|episode=w:The Economy Drive|title=w:Yes, Minister|season=1|number=3|role=w:Jim Hacker; w:Humphrey Appleby|actor=w:Paul Eddington; w:Nigel Hawthorne|passage=Sir Humphrey Appleby: Nevertheless we do not admit it (the existence of [[MI6]]). Not everyone round this table has been '''vetted''' ([[pun]]). / Jim Hacker: I thought that's something you do to cats ([[laugh track]]). / Sir Humphrey Appleby: Yes indeed, but not ferrets, Minister ([[laugh track]]).}}
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=2000
|month=September
|chapter=Corrupt and Farcical Elections
|title=Racial Discrimination in Tibet
|url=https://archive.org/details/nQJE_racial-discrimination-in-tibet-tibetan-centre-for-human-rights-and-democracy/
|publisher=w:Tibetan Centre for Human Rights and Democracy
|OCLC=47965220
|page=116
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/nQJE_racial-discrimination-in-tibet-tibetan-centre-for-human-rights-and-democracy/page/116/
|text=41 year-old Thupten from Golog Tibetan Autonomous Prefecture confirmed this threat of punishment. “The people in my area were very discontent when an election was announced. We knew that the candidates selected had already been carefully '''vetted''' by higher Chinese officials, and that our ‘voting’ was mere lip-service to Chinese claims of democracy, but we did it anyway.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|passage=House and Senate Committees are made up of elected legislators who '''vet''' the bill and hear from stakeholders and members of the public.|url=https://mnnurses.org/issues-advocacy/issues/how-the-legislature-works/how-a-bill-becomes-a-law/|title=How a Bill Becomes a Law|year=2023|work=mnnurses.org|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20231222080523/https://mnnurses.org/issues-advocacy/issues/how-the-legislature-works/how-a-bill-becomes-a-law/|archivedate=22 December 2023}}

