{{also|miss|miß|Miß|miss-|miß-|Miss.}}
==English==
{{wp}}

===Etymology===
From {{m|en|mistress}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|mĭs}}, {{IPA|en|/mɪs/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-Miss.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈmɪz/|a=colloquial}}
*: {{rhymes|en|ɪs}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|+|Mlles}}

# {{n-g|A form of address, now used chiefly for an [[unmarried]] [[woman]]; used chiefly of girls before the mid-1700s, and thereafter used also of adult women without regard to marital status.}}
#* {{RQ:Alcott Little Women|text=Mrs. Gardiner would be happy to see '''Miss''' March and '''Miss''' Josephine at a little dance on New-Year's-Eve.}}
## {{n-g|With a surname.}}
##: {{ux|en|These are the two young ladies that I wanted you to meet, '''Miss''' Jones and '''Miss''' Robinson.}}
## {{n-g|With a full name.}}
##: {{ux|en|The victim was named as '''Miss''' Jane Doe.}}
## {{lb|en|dated|or|regional}} {{n-g|With a first name only.}}
##: {{ux|en|Excuse me Ma'am, will '''Miss''' Julia and '''Miss''' Emily be staying for tea?}}
##* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=6|passage=She was so mad she wouldn't speak to me for quite a spell, but at last I coaxed her into going up to '''Miss''' Emmeline's room and fetching down a tintype of the missing Deacon man.}}
## {{lb|en|dated}} {{n-g|Used alone.}}
##: {{ux|en|Good morning, '''Miss'''. May I help you?}}
# {{n-g|A form of address for a female [[teacher]] or a [[waitress]].}}
#: {{ux|en|Excuse me, '''Miss''', Donny's been pinching my pencils again.}}
# {{n-g|Used in title of the (female) winner of a beauty contest, or certain other types of contest, prefixing the country or other region that she represents, or the category of contest.}}
#: {{ux|en|Ladies and gentlemen, please give a round of applause to our lovely new '''Miss''' Yorkshire!}}
#: {{ux|en|And I can now announce that this year's '''Miss''' Personality is ... Doris Miggins!}}
# {{lb|en|often disparaging or sarcastic}} {{n-g|Used in a mock title to point out some quality, or alleged quality, of a girl or woman.}}
#: {{ux|en|Don't ask me, ask '''Miss''' know-it-all over there.}}

====Usage notes====
* When referring to people with the same name, either of two forms may be used: ''Misses Brown'' or ''Miss Browns''.
* Both ''Miss'' and {{m|en|Mrs}} are frequently replaced by {{m|en|Ms}} in current usage.
* In the US, the use of ''Miss'' with a first name only, as in ''Miss Julia'', was common in the Southern US only. Elsewhere only the full or last names were possible: ''Miss Brown'', ''Miss Julia Brown''. In the UK, ''Miss'' with a first name only was formerly associated with the speech of servants or lower-class persons when addressing or referring to their superiors.
* Traditionally, if a family had multiple unmarried daughters, to avoid ambiguity, only the eldest would be referred to as ''Miss (surname)'', while a younger daughter would be ''Miss (given name)'' or ''Miss (given name) (surname)'' (see Alcott quote above). <ref>Emily Post, ''Etiquette in Society, in Business, in Politics, and at Home'', 1922, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/14314/14314-h/14314-h.htm</ref>

