* {{enPR|răb'ət|a=Australia,weak vowel}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈɹæbət/}}
** {{audio|en|en-au-rabbit.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɾabɪt/|a=Scotland,Wales}}
* {{rhymes|en|æbɪt|s=2}}
* {{homophones|en|rabbet}}

===Etymology 1===
{{inh+|en|enm|rabet}}, {{m|enm|rabette}}, from Anglo-{{der|en|la|rabettus}}, from dialectal {{der|en|fro|rabotte}}, probably a diminutive of {{der|en|dum|-}} or {{der|en|vls|robbe|t=rabbit, seal}}, of {{unc|en|nocap=1}} origin; possibly some {{onomatopoeic|en|title=imitative}} verb, maybe {{m|dum|robben}}, {{m|gml|rubben|t=to rub}} is used here to allude to a characteristic of the animal. See {{m|en|rub}}.

Related forms include {{cog|frm|rabouillet|t=baby rabbit}} and in {{cog|fr|rabot|t=plane}}), coming via {{cog|wa|-}} {{der|en|fro|-}} (reflected nowadays as {{cog|wa|robète|t=rabbit}}), from {{der|en|dum|robbe}} {{gl|"rabbit; seal"; whence Modern {{cog|nl|rob|t=rabbit", also "seal}}}}; also {{cog|gml|robbe}}, {{m|gml|rubbe|t=rabbit}}, and the later {{cog|nds-de|Rubbe}}, {{m|nds-de|Robb|t=seal}}, {{cog|fy|robbe|t=seal}}, {{cog|stq|Rubbe|t=seal}}, {{cog|frr|rob|t=seal}}, borrowed into {{cog|de|Robbe|t=seal}}.

Meant "young rabbit" until the 19th c., when it came to replace the original general term {{m|en|cony}}, owing to the latter's resemblance to and use as a euphemism for {{m|en|cunny}}, "vulva" (compare {{m|en|ass}} and {{m|en|donkey}}).

Note that there is no inherited Germanic word for rabbits, since hares are the only [[leporids]] native to Britain (as with all of Europe outside the {{w|Iberian Peninsula}} and southwest France); rabbits were introduced from France in the late Middle Ages, likely after the {{w|Norman Invasion}}. (Fittingly, {{m|en|hare}} is indeed inherited from Proto-Germanic.)

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{senseid|en|Q9394}}A [[mammal]] of most [[genus|genera]] of the family {{taxfmt|Leporidae|family}}, with long ears, long [[hind leg]]s and a short, [[fluffy]] tail. (''Those of the larger kind are instead called [[hare]]s'') 
#: {{ux|en|The pioneers survived by eating the small game they could get: '''rabbits''', squirrels and occasionally a raccoon.}}
#* {{RQ:Jefferies Amateur Poacher|chapter=The Old Punt: A Curious ‘Turnpike’|pages=19–20|pageref=19|passage=Orion hit a '''rabbit''' once; but though sore wounded it got to the bury, and, struggling in, the arrow caught the side of the hole and was drawn out. Indeed, a nail filed sharp is not of much avail as an arrowhead; you must have it barbed, and that was a little beyond our skill.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The [[meat]] from this animal.
#: {{ux|en|She was cooking '''rabbit''' stew for dinner.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The [[fur]] of a rabbit typically used to imitate another [[animal]]'s fur.
# A [[runner]] in a [[distance]] [[race]] whose goal is mainly to set the [[pace]], either to [[tire]] a specific [[rival]] so that a [[teammate]] can win or to help another break a [[record]]; a [[pacesetter]].
# {{lb|en|cricket}} A very poor [[batsman]], selected as a [[bowler]] or [[wicket-keeper]].
#: {{syn|en|walking wicket}}
# {{lb|en|cricket}} A [[batsman]] who is frequently [[dismiss]]ed by the same [[bowler]] (said to be that player's rabbit).
#: {{syn|en|bunny}}
#: {{ux|en|Glenn McGrath dismissed Michael Atherton a record 19 times; hence Atherton is McGrath's '''rabbit'''.}}
# {{lb|en|comptheory}} A large [[element]] at the beginning of a [[list]] of items to be [[bubble sort]]ed, and thus tending to be quickly swapped into its correct position. Compare {{m|en|turtle}}.
# [[rarebit|Rarebit]]; [[Welsh rarebit|Welsh rabbit]] or a similar dish: melted cheese served atop toast.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2018|author=Helen Saberi|title=Teatimes: A World Tour|publisher=Reaktion Books|isbn=9781780239682
|passage=The cheese mixture may be served with toast on the side or poured on top of toast and grilled until golden brown and bubbling. Other variations include Buck '''rabbit''', a Welsh '''rabbit''' with a poached egg on top, and Yorkshire '''rabbit''' with bacon &nbsp;...}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1858|author=Mrs. N. K. M. Lee|title=The American Family Cook Book|page=47
|passage=CHEESE TOASTED, OR '''RABBIT'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2019|author=Mark Kurlansky|title=Milk: A 10,000-Year History|publisher=Bloomsbury Publishing|isbn=9781526614353
|passage=Hannah Glasse offered recipes for Scotch, Welsh, and English '''rabbit'''. This is her Scotch '''rabbit''': Toast a piece of bread very nicely {{...}} Cut a slice of cheese, {{...}}}}
# A pneumatically-controlled tool used to insert small [[sample]]s of [[material]] inside the [[core]] of a [[nuclear reactor]].
#* '''2012''', Joseph Cerny, ''Nuclear Spectroscopy and Reactions 40-A'' (part 1, page 249)
