## {{senseid|en|Q352388}} A person who drives a motorized vehicle such as a car, truck, bus, train, forklift, etc.
##: {{ux|en|The '''driver''' will make an announcement when the trip is nearing its completion.}}
##: {{ux|en|Tipping your '''driver''' is optional but is much appreciated.}}
##* {{RQ:en:CNN|author=Matt McFarland|title=Why your car’s speedometer goes up to 160 mph (even when your car can’t)|url=https://www.cnn.com/2022/09/10/business/speedometers-160-mph|date=2022-09-10|passage=That’s nearly twice the highest posted speed limit in the United States, 85 mph, on a stretch of highway in Texas. Why do our speedometers stretch to a speed that’s illegal, and only race-car '''drivers''' will ever reach?<br>Toyota spokesman Paul Hogard said the automaker wants speedometers to be easy to read, so there’s value in placing the typical operating speed of American cars, 45 mph to 70 mph, he said, at the top of the speedometer, which is the easiest place on the speedometer for the '''driver''' to read.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260515165213/https://www.cnn.com/2022/09/10/business/speedometers-160-mph|archivedate=15 May 2026}}
##* {{quote-web|en|url=https://westvirginiawatch.com/2024/12/25/self-driving-cars-arent-here-yet-but-states-are-getting-the-rules-ready/|passage=Earlier this month, the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration began an investigation into four crashes of Teslas operating with a partial-automation system (which can navigate highways and steer the car on city streets but requires a licensed '''driver''' to be present), including one in which a pedestrian was killed. In a news release, NHTSA said reduced visibility may have led to the crashes.|author=Elaine S. Povich|title=Self-driving cars aren’t here yet, but states are getting the rules ready|date=2024-12-25|work=w:West Virginia Watch|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260909123502/https://westvirginiawatch.com/2024/12/25/self-driving-cars-arent-here-yet-but-states-are-getting-the-rules-ready/|archivedate=9 September 2026}}
## {{senseid|en|Q2095549}} {{lb|en|aviation|slang}} A [[pilot]] (person who flies aircraft).
## {{senseid|en|Q71052981}} A person who drives [[livestock]]: a [[drover]].
##* {{quote-book |en |year=1920 |last=Cude |first=W.F. |editor=J. Marvin Hunter |chapter=Trail driving to Kansas and elsewhere |title=The Trail Drivers of Texas: Interesting Sketches of Early Cowboys and Their Experiences on the Range and on the Trail During the Days that Tried Men's Souls — True Narratives Related by Real Cowpunchers and Men who Fathered the Cattle Industry in Texas |volume=1 |publisher=George W. Saunders |page=192 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=BaRAAQAAMAAJ&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&dq=%22the%20drivers%22%20%22cattle%22&pg=PA192#v=onepage&q=%22the%20drivers%22%20%22cattle%22&f=false |passage=Up until 1872 there was not over 150 miles of railroad in the state {{quote-gloss|Texas}}, that was from Galveston to Houston, and a short line from Houston to Brazoria, twenty-five miles in length, and one road from Harrisburg to Alton, three miles east of Columbus. So the cattle driving to Kansas was the only hope at that time, and it proved to be a great help before the railroads got around. Trail driving to Kansas lasted from 1866 to 1886 and it was estimated that fully eight million head of cattle and horses were driven and sold during the twenty years above mentioned to Kansas, the '''drivers''' paying for the cattle on an average of $10.00 per head, although most of the horses came back to Texas and were used the next year. There were all sizes of herds from five hundred to twenty-five hundred cattle in a drove, usually seven or eight men to the small herds and twelve to fifteen men with the large herds.}}
# Something that [[drive#Verb|drive]]s something else.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014|author=Bridgette Wessels|title=Exploring Social Change: Process and Context|page=106
|passage=The character of work is a '''driver''' of social change, at the same time that any new forms of work are the result of broader social change.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2020 December 16|title=Network News: "Robust case" for Fawley branch reopening|journal=Rail|page=14|text=The aim is to secure up to £140 million for the combined road and rail improvements, including a new road bridge to replace a level crossing at [[Totton]]. A key '''driver''' has been the approval of a new housing and employment development called [[Fawley]] Waterside, with 1,500 homes planned on the site of a redundant power station on the edge of [[Southampton Water]].}}
