#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1989|month=fall|author=w:Allan Gurganus|title=A Hog Loves Its Life|editor=w:Gordon Lish|journal=w:The Quarterly|issue=11|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Vintage Books|page=118|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/isbn_9780679721734/page/118/mode/1up|isbn=0-679-72173-8|issn=0893-3103|passage=At this the farmer gets to breathing '''funnier''', hiccuping, little stray burps roll out—uncontrolled, we’re talking here.}}
# {{lb|en|Jamaica|offensive|derogatory}} In a manner seen as being typical of a [[homosexual]], or indicating [[homosexuality]].
#* {{quote-song|en|year=2002|author=Sean Paul|title=Like Glue|album=Dutty Rock (album title)|url=https://genius.com/Sean-paul-like-glue-lyrics
|passage=Dem nuh waan nuh honey, dem only waan di money. Dat’s how me know seh dem bwoy deh all a move '''funny'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2017|author=Christina Williams|chapter=The Bullied Girl In the Mall|title=Scribble: A Novel|page=221|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/scribblenovel0000will/page/221/mode/1up|isbn=978-1974648429|passage=He walked away, pushing the napkin in his pink pants pockets. By the way, he dresses and walks '''funnier''' out of school. He likes tight feminine colored clothes and walks like he is holding a stick between his butt cheeks. You probably wouldn’t notice him if you didn't hear his voice and looked him in the eye. Jenna assumed he liked men.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=2018|author=Jah Lando|title=Money Hard|url=https://genius.com/Jah-lando-money-hard-lyrics
|passage=Nuff boy move '''funny''' just fi get money.}}

===Etymology 2===
Perhaps a jocular use of {{m|en|funny}}. See above.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|UK|Irish|watercraft|dated}} A narrow, [[clinker-built]] [[double-ender]] for [[scull]]ing.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1810|title=Letters from a Nobleman to His Son, During the Period of His Education at Eton and Oxford|volume=1|pages=63–64|pageurl=https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=VGCOlviYjHsC&pg=PA63|text=I myself recollect the '''''funnies''''' at Eton, which were certain little boats, capable of containing one person only, who, like an Indian in his canoe, was constantly obliged to balance himself, with such critical nicety, that the least mistake was certain of producing a ducking.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=12 December 1841|author={{w|Lord Kelvin|William Thomson}}|chapter=[Letter to father]|mainauthor=w:Silvanus P. Thompson|title=The Life of William Thomson, Baron Kelvin of Largs|volume=1|year_published=1910|page=33|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/lifeofwillthom01thomrich/page/n60/mode/1up|text=As I do not belong to the boat-club, I always row by myself in a '''funny''' {{...}} or at least go in a two-oared boat, with some friend with whom I should otherwise be walking.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1971|author=Julian Tyndale-Biscoe|title=Gunner Subaltern|page=144|text=We were going to have a go in one of the double scull ‘'''funnies'''’ that could be had from the Rowing Club in the harbour, but he is off up the line tomorrow to rejoin his old Battery.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1999|author={{w|Patrick Wright (historian)|Patrick Wright}}|title=The River: The Thames in Our Time|isbn=0563384786|page=145|text=There was a great market for leisure boats, too: no [[wherry|wherries]] any longer, but whole fleets of [[punt]]s, rowing [[gig#Etymology 3|gigs]], '''funnies''', [[skiff]]s, [[whiff]]s and [[randan]]s {{...}}}}

{{C|en|Comedy|Personality}}

clever.content:
{{also|Clever}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From [[East Anglia]]n dialectal English {{m|en|cliver||expert at seizing}}, from {{inh|en|enm|cliver||tenacious}}.

* perhaps from {{inh|en|ang|*clifer}}, {{m|ang|clibbor||clinging}};
* or perhaps from {{der|en|nl|-}}, {{der|en|nds|-}}, or East/{{der|en|stq|-}} (compare {{m|stq|kluftich|t=clever, prudent}}, probably derived from {{der|en|gmw-pro|*kleuban|t=to cleave, split}});
* or dialectal {{der|en|no|klover||ready, skillful}}, itself borrowed from {{der|en|gml|klever}}, related to {{m|gml|kleven|t=to stick}}, from {{der|en|osx| klibōn}}, from {{der|en|gmw-pro|*klibēn}}, related to the Old English word above;
* possibly influenced by {{cog|ang|clifer||claw, hand}} (compare {{m|ang|clawian|t=to claw}}).<ref>{{R:Etymonline}}</ref>

Related to {{l|en|cleave}}. Perhaps influenced by {{cog|cy|celfydd||talented, dexterous, expert}}.

Compare typologically {{m+|cs|chytrý}}, {{m+|ru|хи́трый}} (akin to {{m|ru|хвата́ть}}), also note {{m|ru|схва́тывать на лету́}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈklɛv.ə/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈklɛv.ɚ/|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-clever.ogg|a=US}}
* {{hyph|en|clev|er}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛvə(ɹ)|s=2}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er,more}}

# [[nimble|Nimble]] with hands or body; dexterous; [[skillful]]; [[adept]].
#: ''My dad is quite '''clever''' with his hands, especially at carpentry''.
#* {{ante|1898}}, {{w|Francis James Child}} (collator), ''[[s:Child's Ballads/198|Child's Ballads]]'', 198: "Bonny John Seton",
#*: The Highland men, they're '''clever''' men <br> At handling sword and shield,
# Quick to understand, learn, and devise or apply ideas; [[intelligent]].
#* {{RQ:Economist|title=German recreation: An affinity for rules?|url=http://www.economist.com/node/12009728|date=28 August 2008
|passage=The '''cleverest''' and most prolific inventors, such as Reiner Knizia (who lives in England) are nerdy superstars. Euro (also “German-style”) games must not be confused with “Ameritrash” games, which generally involve high drama and employ plastic pieces, though arguing over what the difference is seems to be gamers' second-favourite pastime.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20170731150354/http://www.economist.com/node/12009728|archivedate=31 July 2017}}
# Mentally quick and [[resourceful]]; skilled at achieving what one wants in a mentally agile and inventive way.
#: {{ux|en|'''clever''' like a fox}}
#: {{ux|en|With a '''clever''' lawyer, she could easily be acquitted.}}
#* '''1890''', {{w|Joseph Jacobs}} (collator), ''[[s:English Fairy Tales/Molly Whuppie|Molly Whuppie]]'', ''English Fairy Tales'',
#*: The youngest of the three strange lassies was called Molly Whuppie, and she was very '''clever'''. She noticed that before they went to bed the giant put straw ropes round her neck and her sisters', and round his own lassies' necks, he put gold chains. So Molly took care and did not fall asleep, but waited till she was sure every one was sleeping sound. Then she slipped out of the bed, and took the straw ropes off her own and her sisters' necks, and took the gold chains off the giant's lassies. She then put the straw ropes on the giant's lassies and the gold on herself and her sisters, and lay down.
# [[smart|Smart]], [[intelligent]], or [[witty]]; mentally [[quick]] or [[sharp]].
#* '''19th c''', {{w|Charles Kingsley}}, ''A Farewell'',
#*: Be good, sweet maid, and let who will be '''clever'''; <br> Do noble things, not dream them all day long: <br> And so make life, death, and that vast forever / One grand, sweet song.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1860|author=w:John Timbs|title=School-Days of Eminent Men|page=177|url=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=_QEUAAAAIAAJ&pg=PA177&dq=%22Though+there+were+many+clever+men+in+England%22+-intitle:%22%22&hl=en&ei=XNDDTqamEuyHmQWR46GHCw&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=1&ved=0CDIQ6AEwAA#v=onepage&q=%22Though%20there%20were%20many%20clever%20men%20in%20England%22%20-intitle%3A%22%22&f=false|passage=[[w:Thomas Babington Macaulay|Lord Macaulay]] has said of Bunyan: “though there were many '''clever''' men in England during the latter half of the seventeenth century, there were only two great creative minds. One of these minds produced ‘The Paradise Lost;’ the other, ‘The Pilgrim's Progress.’”}}
#* '''1912''', {{w|Fyodor Dostoevsky}}, {{w|Constance Garnett}} (translator), ''[[s:The Brothers Karamazov/Book V/Chapter 7|The Brothers Karamazov]]'', Book V, Chapter 7: "It's Always Worth While Speaking to a '''Clever''' Man",
#*: I would have sent Alyosha, but what use is Alyosha in a thing like that? I send you just because you are a '''clever''' fellow. Do you suppose I don't see that? You know nothing about timber, but you've got an eye.
# {{lb|en|archaic}} {{cap|sane}}; in one's right mind.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1892|author=Margaret Oliphant|title=The Heir Presumptive and the Heir Apparent|page=263
|passage=He was not '''clever''', poor fellow, he did not know what questions to ask; he asked the same questions again and again. He continued to show his own troubled thoughts, and the vague dread in his mind, {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|of objects or actions}} Showing mental quickness and resourcefulness.
#: {{ux|en|This is a simple but '''clever''' trick to solve the problem.}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|date=2013-06-22|volume=407|issue=8841|page=70
|title=Engineers of a different kind
|url=http://www.economist.com/news/finance-and-economics/21579879-buy-out-firm-really-does-focus-operational-improvements-engineers
|passage=Private-equity nabobs bristle at being dubbed mere financiers.{{...}}Much of their pleading is public-relations bluster. '''Clever''' financial ploys are what have made billionaires of the industry’s veterans. “Operational improvement” in a portfolio company has often meant little more than promising colossal bonuses to sitting chief executives if they meet ambitious growth targets. That model is still prevalent today.}}
# {{lb|en|of objects or actions}} Showing [[inventiveness]] or [[originality]]; [[witty]].
#* {{RQ:Austen Emma|volume=1|chapter=9|passage=Mr. Woodhouse was almost as much interested in the business as the girls, and tried very often to recollect something worth their putting in. "So many '''clever''' riddles as there used to be when he was young--he wondered he could not remember them! but he hoped he should in time." And it always ended in "Kitty, a fair but frozen maid."}}
#* {{RQ:Maugham Moon and Sixpence|chapter=III|passage=I felt they expected me to say '''clever''' things, and I never could think of any till after the party was over.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=April 10, 2011|author=Alistair Magowan|work=BBC Sport
|title=Aston Villa 1-0 Newcastle
|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/eng_prem/9446263.stm
|passage=Just before the break Villa were denied a second goal when Bent had the ball in the net, although he was ruled offside after Jean Makoun's '''clever''' pass.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2014-04-11|author=[http://www.washingtonpost.com/pb/ron-charles Ron Charles]
|volume=190|issue=18|page=37|magazine=w:The Guardian Weekly
|title=David Grand’s ‘Mount Terminus’
|url=http://www.washingtonpost.com/entertainment/books/david-grands-mount-terminus-reviewed-by-ron-charles/2014/03/18/6bdce9be-a98f-11e3-b61e-8051b8b52d06_story.html
|passage=The Rosenbloom Loop is a '''clever''' little device, but it’s an even more '''clever''' symbol of the role that discipline plays in the creation of illusion: the persistence of vision that makes sequential still images appear to move.}}
# {{lb|en|UK|colloquial|chiefly in the negative}} Fit and healthy; free from fatigue or illness.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=Colin Ross|title=Death of the Docks|page=196|passage=But at that moment I knew it was all over for me, I had never thought that this day would come, but it had and I was not feeling too '''clever'''. In fact I had to escape to a nearby toilet to be sick.}}
#* {{quote-av|en|year=2019|author=Neil Forsyth|actor=Jamie Sives|role=Jake McCall|title=Guilt|season=1|number=1|time=30:07|text=Right, and no, because the solicitor has told her that Walter's legs didn't look too '''clever''', which, apparently, everyone's putting down to a fall, thankfully.}}
# {{lb|en|US|dated}} [[good-natured|Good-natured]]; [[obliging]].
# {{lb|en|anthropology|of an Aboriginal Australian}} Possessing [[magical]] abilities.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1904|title=Journal & Proceedings of the Royal Society of New South Wales|volume=XXXVIII|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=oxEWAQAAIAAJ&q=%22When+a+clever+man+is+out+hunting%22+-intitle:%22%22&dq=%22When+a+clever+man+is+out+hunting%22+-intitle:%22%22&hl=en&ei=zMnDTsyVL63NmAXKx9iUCw&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&redir_esc=y|page=255
|passage=When a '''clever''' man is out hunting and comes across the tracks of, say, a kangaroo, he follows them along and talks to the footprints all the time for the purpose of injecting magic into the animal which made them.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1947|journal=Oceania,|volume=16-17|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=KcUZAAAAMAAJ&q=%22The+two+women,+who+were+clever%22+-intitle:%22%22&dq=%22The+two+women,+who+were+clever%22+-intitle:%22%22&hl=en&ei=zsvDTvudHqGJmQWawIjnCg&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=3&ved=0CDwQ6AEwAg|page=330
|passage=Prior to this, the two women, who were “'''clever''',” and possessed a certain amount of magical “power,”{{...}}.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1991|author=John & Sue Erbacher|title=Aborigines of the Rainforest|passage=Fred is the '''clever''' fellow or tribal doctor who practises with the Kuku-Yalanji people. The tribal doctor’s work includes curing sickness, finding out the causes of death, predicting the future and making and stopping rain.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[fit|Fit]]; [[suitable]]; having [[propriety]].
#* '''18th c''', {{w|Jonathan Swift}}, {{w|Alexander Pope}} (later lines), ''Imitation of Horace'', 1852, {{w|Charles Knight (publisher)|Charles Knight}} (collator), ''Half-hours with the Best Authors'', Volume 4, [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=f_0pAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA188&dq=%22sound+more+clever+/+To+me+and+to+my+heirs+forever%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwj8rYuotpvaAhUn9YMKHZPsB-gQ6AEIQjAF#v=onepage&q=%22sound%20more%20clever%20%2F%20To%20me%20and%20to%20my%20heirs%20forever%22&f=false page 188],
#*: I can't but think 'twould sound more '''clever''', <br> To me and to my heirs forever.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Well-shaped; [[handsome]].
#* {{RQ:Arbuthnot Law|part=3|chapter=The Character of John Bull's Sister Peg, with the Quarrels that Happen'd between Master and Miss, in Their Childhood|page=10|passage=Tho' the Girl vvas a tight, '''clever''' VVench as any vvas, and thro' her pale Looks, you might diſcern Spirit and Vivacity, vvhich made her not indeed a perfect Beauty, but ſomething that vvas agreeable.}}

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|smart, intelligent or witty}} {{l|en|quick-witted}}, {{l|en|sharp-witted}}
** See also [[Thesaurus:intelligent]]
* {{sense|resourceful, perhaps cunning}} {{l|en|cunning}}, {{l|en|street-smart}}
* {{sense|nimble or skillful}} {{l|en|adroit}}, {{l|en|talented}}
* {{sense|showing inventiveness}} {{l|en|ingenious}}
* {{sense|possessing magical powers}}  {{l|en|crafty}}, {{l|en|cunning}}, {{l|en|magical}}

