scary.content:
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|scarey}} {{q|dated}}

===Etymology 1===
{{ety|en|:af|scare|-y<id:adjectival>|text=+|tree=1}}

====Pronunciation====
* {{enPR|skâr'ē|a=RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈskɛə.ɹi/|/ˈskɛː.ɹi/}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈskɛə.ɹi/|/ˈskɛɹ.i/|a=GA}}
** {{audio|en|En-us-scary.ogg|a=US}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈskeːɹi/|a=AU}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈskeəɹi/|a=NZ,non-near-square}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈskiəɹi/|a=NZ,near-square}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈskeɹɪ/|/ˈskeɹe/|a=Scotland}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈskɜːɹɪ/|a=Lancashire,square-nurse}}
** {{homophones|en|skerry}} {{qualifier|only in accents with the Mary-marry-merry merger}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛəɹi|s=2}}

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|er}}

# {{lb|en|now|_|chiefly|informal}} Causing [[fear]] or [[anxiety]]
#: {{syn|en|frightening|hair-raising|petrifying|terrifying|Thesaurus:frightening}}
#: {{ux|en|The tiger's jaws were '''scary'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|She was hiding behind her pillow during the '''scary''' parts of the film.}}
#: {{ux|en| He watched Conjuring but it was too '''scary''' for him.}}
#* {{RQ:Twain Huckleberry Finn|chapter=29|text=Well, we swarmed along down the river road, just carrying on like wildcats; and to make it more '''scary''' the sky was darking up, and the lightning beginning to wink and flitter, and the wind to shiver amongst the leaves.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1982|author=w:Anne Tyler|title=w:Dinner at the Homesick Restaurant|location=New York|publisher=Ivy Books|year_published=1992|chapter=2|page=70|url=https://archive.org/details/dinnerathomesick00anne|text=“{{...}} How '''scary''' it is to know that everyone I love depends on me! I’m afraid I’ll do something wrong.”}}
#* {{RQ:en:New York Times|date=02-09-2007|author=Terrence Rafferty|title=Technicolor Dreamboat|url=http://www.nytimes.com/2007/09/02/books/review/Rafferty-t.html
|passage=Age notwithstanding, none of these men seem interested in sailing to Byzantium anyway: Justine’s got them all on the last stage to someplace wilder and '''scarier''', where Yeats’s “monuments of unaging intellect” are thoroughly beside the point.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20210126120351/https://www.nytimes.com/2007/09/02/books/review/Rafferty-t.html|archivedate=26 January 2021}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2020|author=Lesley L. Smith|title=A Jack For All Seasons|page=45
|passage=The biggest, '''scariest''', scarriest man in the room said, “It's about time, Jones.”}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} Uncannily [[striking]] or [[surprising]].
#: {{ux|en|Linda changed her hair, and it’s '''scary''' how much she looks like her mother.}}
# {{lb|en|US|colloquial}} Subject to sudden [[alarm]]; easily [[frighten]]ed.
#: {{syn|en|nervous|jumpy}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1823|author=w:James Fenimore Cooper|title={{w|The Pioneers (novel)|The Pioneers}}|location=New York|publisher=Charles Wiley|volume=2|chapter=5|page=77|url=https://archive.org/details/pioneersorsource02cooprich|text=“Whist! whist!” said Natty, in a low voice, on hearing a slight sound made by Elizabeth, in bending over the side of the canoe, in eager curiosity; “’tis a '''sceary''' animal, and it’s a far stroke for a spear. {{...}}”|note=the UK edition of the same year has'' '''scary''' ''(p.{{nbsp}}262)''<sup>[https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/100591073]</sup>}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1867|author=w:John Greenleaf Whittier|section=The Wreck of Rivermouth|title=The Tent on the Beach, and Other Poems|location=Boston|publisher=Ticknor and Fields|page=25|url=https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/004437740|text=“She’s cursed,” said the skipper; “speak her fair:<br>I’m '''scary''' always to see her shake<br>Her wicked head, with its wild gray hair,<br>And nose like a hawk, and eyes like a snake.”}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1916|author=Texas Department of Agriculture|title=Bulletin|issue=47-57|page=150|text=And let us say to these interests that, until the Buy-It-Made-In-Texas movement co-operates with the farmers, we are going to be a little '''scary''' of the snare.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1940|author={{w|Richard Wright (author)|Richard Wright}}|title=w:Native Son|location=London|publisher=Jonathan Cape|year_published=1970|volume_plain=book 1|page=10|url=https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.499539|text=The two brothers stood over the dead rat {{...}}.<br>“Please, Bigger, take ’im out,” Vera begged.<br>“Aw, don’t be so '''scary''',” Buddy said.}}

