# {{senseid|en|act as chairperson}} {{lb|en|transitive}} To act as [[chairperson]] at; to [[preside]] over.
#: {{ux|en|Bob will '''chair''' tomorrow's meeting.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2000|author=David Golinkin|title=Responsa in a Moment|volume=3|chapter=Institutionalizing Parents With Alzheimer's Disease|page=38|text=Dama ben Netina, the gentile "Mayor" of first-century Ashkelon, was once '''chairing''' a meeting of the City Council.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2020 May 20|title=Merriman praised over handling of TSC's 'virtual' transition|journal=Rail|page=12|text=Greenwood told ''RAIL'' she was disappointed that Parliamentary rules prevented her from '''chairing''' the TSC <small>[Transport Select Committee]</small> beyond last December's General Election, [...] She added: "I'm gutted I'm no longer able to '''chair''' the committee, I'm not going to lie. But I know it's in good hands and I'm still able to play my part as a member in the work we're doing.}}
# {{senseid|en|carry on shoulders}} {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[carry]] in a [[seated]] [[position]] upon one's [[shoulders]], especially in [[celebration]] or [[victory]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1896|author=w:A. E. Houseman|chapter=To An Athlete Dying Young,|title=A Shropshire Lad
|passage=The time you won your town the race<br>We '''chaired''' you through the marketplace.}}
# {{senseid|en|give award for poetry}} {{lb|en|transitive|Wales|UK}} To [[award]] a chair to (a winning [[poet]]) at a [[Welsh]] [[eisteddfod]].
#: {{ux|en|The poet was '''chaired''' at the national Eisteddfod.}}

