==English==
{{was wotd|2022|August|19}}

===Etymology===
{{PIE word|en|dwís}}{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₃reǵ-}}
{{dercat|en|la|itc-pro}}
{{etymon|en|:inh|enm:dressen|tree=1}}
The {{glossary|verb}} is from {{inh|en|enm|dressen}}, {{m|enm|dresse|t=to arrange, put in order}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=dressen|pos=v|id=MED12602}}</ref> from {{der|en|xno|-}}, {{der|en|fro|dresser}}, {{m|fro|drecier}} (modern {{cog|fr|dresser}}), from {{der|en|la-lat|*dīrēctiāre|t=to guide, direct}}, from {{der|en|la-cla|dīrēctus}}, whence {{m+|en|direct}}. Further akin to {{m+|la|regō}}.

The {{glossary|noun}} is derived from the verb.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=57671|date=September 2021|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=n}}</ref>

Compare typologically {{m|en|adorn}} (<< {{m+|la|ōrnō}} < {{m|la|ōrdō}}, whence also {{m|la|ōrdinō}}, {{m+|en|order}}, {{m|en|ornament}}); {{m+|ru|наряжа́ть}}, {{m|ru|наря́д}} (akin to {{m|ru|ряд}}, {{m|ru|поря́док}}).

