===Verb===
{{en-verb|+|+|+|+,drest<l:obsolete>}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}}
## {{lb|en|also|reflexive|and|figuratively}} To [[put#Verb|put]] [[clothes]] (or, formerly, [[armour#Noun|armour]]) on (oneself or someone, a [[doll#Noun|doll]], a [[mannequin]], etc.); to [[clothe]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##: {{synonyms|en|attire|don|Thesaurus:clothe}}
##: {{antonyms|en|strip|undress|Thesaurus:undress}}
##: {{ux|en|He was '''dressed''' in the latest fashions.}}
##: {{ux|en|My kids are old enough to '''dress''' themselves now.}}
##* {{RQ:Beaumont Fletcher Woman Hater|scene=iv|page=50|passage=O rich! rich! vvhere ſhould I get clothes to '''dreſſe''' her in?}}
##* {{RQ:More Psychodia|chapter=Psychozoia|book=3|stanza=56|page=51|passage=Their face with love and vigour vvas '''ydreſt''', / VVith modeſty and joy, their tongue with juſt beheſt.}}
##* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=II|page=15|passage=Sunning himself on the board steps, I saw for the first time Mr. Farquhar Fenelon Cooke. He was '''dressed''' out in broad gaiters and bright tweeds, like an English tourist, and his face might have belonged to Dagon, idol of the Philistines.}}
##* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=Justifiably Angry Young Men|page=93|passage=I remember a lady coming to inspect St. Mary's Home where I was brought up and seeing us all in our lovely Elizabethan uniforms we were so proud of, and bursting into tears all over us because "it was wicked to '''dress''' us like charity children". We nearly crowned her we were so offended.}}
### {{lb|en|specifically}} To [[attire#Verb|attire]] (oneself or someone) for a [[particular#Adjective|particular]] (especially [[formal#Adjective|formal]]) [[occasion#Noun|occasion]], or in a [[fashionable]] [[manner]].
###* {{RQ:Sidney Arcadia|chapter=6|page=40|passage=[A]ll the men there shoulde '''dresse''' themselves like the poorest sorte of the people in ''Arcadia'', having no banners, but bloudie shirtes hanged upon long staves, {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:Burton Melancholy|edition=2nd|chapter=Artificiall Allurements of Loue, Causes and Provocations to Lust. Gestures, Cloathes, Dowre &c.|partition=3|section=2|member=2|subsection=3|page=376|pageref=475|passage={{...}} ''Anthony'' [''i.e.'', {{w|Mark Antony}}] himſelfe was quite beſotted with ''{{w|Cleopatra}}''’s ſweete ſpeeches, philters, beauty, pleaſing tires: for when ſhe ſailed along the riuer ''Cydnus'', with ſuch incredible pompe in a guilded ſhip, her ſelfe '''dreſſed''' like ''[[w:Venus (mythology)|Venus]]'', her maides like the ''[[w:Charites|Graces]]'', her Pages like ſo many ''Cupids'', ''Anthony'' was amazed, & rapt beyond himſelfe.}}
###* {{RQ:Pepys Diary|volume=VI|date=25 March 1667|page=238|passage=[H]e and I {{...}} to the King's playhouse; and by and by comes Mr. Lowther and his wife and mine, and into a box, forsooth, neither of them being '''dressed''', which I was almost ashamed of.}}
###* {{RQ:Vanbrugh Relapse|page=29|passage=Naw if I find 'tis a good day, I reſalve to take a turn in the Park, and ſee the Fine Women: So huddle on my Cloaths, and get '''dreſt''' by One.}}
###* {{RQ:Spectator|issue=2|date=2 March 1711|page=88|passage=[B]eing ill-used by the above-mentioned widow, he was very serious for a year and a half; and though, his temper being naturally jovial, he at last got over it, he grew careless of himself, and never '''dressed''' afterwards.}}
###* {{RQ:Fielding Tom Jones|volume=VI|book=XVIII|chapter=The History Draws Nearer to a Conclusion|page=279|passage=[T]he Hour appointed by Mr. ''Weſtern'' now drew ſo near, that he had barely Time left to '''dreſs''' himſelf.}}
###* {{RQ:Goldsmith Citizen of the World|volume=I|chapter=Letter XIV. From the Same [From Lien Chi Altangi, to Fum Hoam, First President of the Ceremonial Academy at Pekin, in China].|pages=48–49|pageref=48|passage=As I was '''dreſſed''' after the faſhion of Europe, ſhe had taken me for an Engliſhman, and conſequently ſaluted me in her ordinary manner; but when the footman informed her grace that I was the gentleman from China, ſhe inſtantly lifted herſelf from the couch, while her eyes ſparkled with unuſual vivacity.}}
## To [[design#Verb|design]], [[make#Verb|make]], [[provide]], or [[select#Verb|select]] clothes (for someone).
##: {{ux|en|The fashion designer was proud to have '''dressed''' the queen for the charity event.}}
## To [[arrange]] or [[style#Verb|style]] (someone's [[hair#Noun|hair]]).
##* {{RQ:Camden Holland Britain|chapter=Romans in Britaine|page=61|passage=&#91;{{w|Domitian}}&#93; after his manner, with a cheerfull countenance and grieved heart, received the newes: being inwardly pricked, to think that his later counterfet triumph of Germany, wherin certain ſlaves bought for mony were attired and their haire '''dreſſed''' as captives of that country, was had in deriſion and iuſtly skorned abroad: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Pepys Diary|volume=III|date=13 July 1663|page=208|passage=By and by the King and Queen, who looked in this dress (a white laced waistcoat and a crimson short pettycoat, and her hair '''dressed''' ''à la negligence'') mighty pretty; and the King rode hand in hand with her.}}
## {{lb|en|also|figuratively}} To [[adorn]] or [[ornament#Verb|ornament]] (something). {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|It was time to '''dress''' the windows for Christmas again.}}
##* {{RQ:Tennyson Poems 1842|volume=I|poem=The Merman|pages=68–69|pageref=69|passage=But at night I would roam abroad and play / With the mermaids in and out of the rocks, / '''Dressing''' their hair with the white sea-flower, / And holding them back by their flowing locks {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Froude Carlyle|volume=II|chapter=A.D. 1834. Aet. 39.|page=400|passage=There was no occasion for [[w:Francis Jeffrey, Lord Jeffrey|[Francis] Jeffrey]] to have written with such extreme harshness. If he felt obliged to expostulate, he might have '''dressed''' his censures in a kinder form.}}
##* {{quote-song|en|author=w:Jay Livingston; w:Ray Evans|artist={{w|Bing Crosby}} and {{w|Carol Richards}}, with {{w|John Scott Trotter}} and His Orchestra and the Lee Gordon Singers|title=w:Silver Bells|month=October|year=1950|passage=City sidewalks, busy sidewalks, '''dressed''' in holiday style / In the air there's a feeling of Christmas}}
### To arrange a [[display#Noun|display]] of [[goods]] in, or to [[decorate]] (a [[shop#Noun|shop]] or [[shop window]]).
### {{lb|en|nautical}} To ornament (a [[ship#Noun|ship]]) by [[hoist#Verb|hoisting]] the [[national#Adjective|national]] [[colour#Noun|colours]] at the [[peak#Noun|peak]] and [[masthead]]s, and [[set#Verb|setting]] the [[jack#Noun|jack]] [[forward#Preposition|forward]]; when "dressed full", the [[signal#Noun|signal]] [[flag#Noun|flags]] and [[pennant]]s are added.
## {{senseid|en|treat_wound}} To [[apply]] a [[dressing#Noun|dressing]] to or otherwise [[treat#Verb|treat]] (a [[wound#Noun|wound]]); {{lb|en|obsolete}} to [[give#Verb|give]] (a [[wounded#Adjective|wounded]] [[person#Noun|person]]) [[medical#Adjective|medical]] [[aid#Noun|aid]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##: {{synonyms|en|bandage|[[put#Verb|put]] a [[bandage#Noun|bandage]] on}}
##* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=III|canto=V|stanza=41|page=473|passage=[T]he Mayd / His readie vvound with better [[salves|ſalues]] new '''dreſt''', / Daily ſhe '''dreſſed''' him, and did the beſt / His grieuous hurt to guariſh, that ſhe might, {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Stevenson Treasure Island|chapter=In the Enemy's Camp|page=227|passage=[H]e was deadly pale, and the blood-stained bandage round his head told that he had recently been wounded, and still more recently '''dressed'''.}}
##* {{RQ:Grey Riders of the Purple Sage|chapter=Shadows on the Sage-slope|page=203|passage=She ran into the house and returned with bandages; and while she bathed and '''dressed''' the wound Lassiter talked.}}
## To [[fit#Verb|fit]] or [[prepare]] (something) for [[use#Noun|use]]; to [[render#Verb|render]] (something) [[suitable]] for an [[intended#Adjective|intended]] [[purpose#Noun|purpose]]; to [[get ready]].
##* {{RQ:Sylvester Du Bartas|year=1611|part=Judith|book=3|page=33|passage=Now when the towne his ſommonds did diſdain, / To conquer it perforce he plyde his pain: / And their, th’''Inginers'' haue the ''Trepan'' '''dreſt''', / And reared vp the ''Ramme'' for batterie beſt: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:King James Version|Exodus|30|7|page=170|column=1|passage=And {{w|Aaron}} ſhall burne thereon [''i.e.'', on the altar of incense] ſweet incenſe euery morning: when he '''dreſſeth''' the lamps he ſhal burn incenſe vpon it.}}
##* {{RQ:Dryden Aeneis|book=7|page=411|lines=378–379|passage=Three hundred Horſes, in high Stables fed, / Stood ready, ſhining all, and ſmoothly '''dreſſ'd'''; {{...}}|footer=That is, [[curry#Verb|curried]] or [[rub#Verb|rubbed]].}}
### To prepare, treat, or [[curry#Verb|curry]] ([[animal]] [[hide#Noun|hide]] or [[leather#Noun|leather]]).
###* {{RQ:Topsell Foure-footed Beastes|chapter=Of the Dogge|page=143|passage=The ſkinnes of Dogges are '''dreſſed''' for gloues, and cloſe Bootes, the vvhich are vſed by ſuch as haue vicerous and ſvvelling Legges or Limbes, for by them the aflicted place receiueth a double reliefe; firſt, it reſiſteth the influent humors, and ſecondly, it is not exaſperated with VVoollen.}}
###* {{RQ:Boswell Johnson|volume=II|year=1776|page=35|passage=Very little buſineſs appeared to be going forward in Lichfield. I found however two ſtrange manufactures for ſo inland a place, ſail-cloth and ſtreamers for ſhips; and I obſerved them making ſome ſaddle-cloths, and '''dreſſing''' ſheepſkins; but upon the whole, the buſy hand of induſtry ſeemed to be quite ſlackened.}}
###* {{RQ:Grey Riders of the Purple Sage|chapter=Silver Spruce and Aspens|page=115|passage=He skinned the rabbits, and gave the dogs the one they had quarreled over, and the skin of this he '''dressed''' and hung up to dry, feeling that he would like to keep it. It was a particularly rich, furry pelt with a beautiful white tail.}}
### {{senseid|en|prepare via breaking, sorting, etc}} {{lb|en|especially of [[ore]]s}} To prepare by any of many types of physical [[processing]] (e.g., breaking, crushing, sorting, sieving, controlled burning or heating).
###: {{hypo|en|rag<id:break ore into lumps for sorting>}}
###: {{ux|en|in mining and metallurgy, to '''dress''' ores by sorting and separating them}}
### {{senseid|en|prepare a surface}} To prepare the [[surface#Noun|surface]] of (a [[material#Noun|material]], such as [[lumber#Noun|lumber]] or [[stone#Noun|stone]]; a [[grindstone]] or [[grinding wheel]]).
###: {{hypo|en|rag<id:do roughing cuts>}}
### {{lb|en|historical|or|England|regional}} To [[remove#Verb|remove]] [[chaff#Noun|chaff]] or [[impurity|impurities]] from ([[flour#Noun|flour]], [[grain#Noun|grain]], etc.) by [[bolt#Verb|bolting]] or [[sift#Verb|sifting]], [[winnow#Verb|winnowing]], and other [[method]]s.
### {{lb|en|fishing}} To prepare (an [[artificial]] [[fly#Noun|fly]]) to be [[attach]]ed to a [[fishhook|fish hook]].
## {{lb|en|agriculture|horticulture}} To [[cultivate]] or [[tend]] to (a [[garden#Noun|garden]], [[land#Noun|land]], [[plant#Noun|plants]], etc.); especially, to [[add#Verb|add]] [[fertilizer]] or [[manure#Noun|manure]] to ([[soil#Noun|soil]]); to [[fertilize]], to [[manure#Verb|manure]].
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 2 Q1|act=III|scene=iv|page=59|passage=''Gard''[''ener'']. Oh what pitie is it that he had not ſo trimde, / And '''dreſt''' his land as vve this garden at time of yeare / Do vvound the barke, the skinne of our fruit trees, {{...}} ''Queene''. Oh I am preſt to death through vvant of ſpeaking / Thou old [[w:Adam|Adams]] likeneſſe ſet to '''dreſſe''' this garden, / How dares thy harſh rude tong ſound this vnpleaſing nevvs?}}
## {{lb|en|[[butchering#Noun|butchering]]}} To [[cut up]] (an animal or its [[flesh#Noun|flesh]]) for food.
##: {{hypo|en|dress out|field dress}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1850|author=w:Thomas Keightley|title=The Fairy Mythology|location=London|publisher=H.G. Bohn|page=534|passage="I'll be with you," said Coo, "never fear me." Jack went home and '''dressed''' a noble fish dinner, and got out plenty of his best foreign spirits, enough for that matter to make twenty men drunk.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=December 2020|author=Tim Folger|title=North America’s most valuable resource is at risk|journal=w:National Geographic Magazine|url=https://www.nationalgeographic.com/magazine/article/north-americas-most-valuable-resource-is-at-risk-feature#:~:text=For%20the%20Anishinaabe%2C%20hunting%20has,share%20with%20family%20and%20friends.|passage=But as he '''dressed''' the carcass—cutting it up to bring home—Borg’s gratitude gave way to revulsion. When he tried to extract the liver, which should have been firm and meaty, it deliquesced into a bloody sludge, sliding goopily through his fingers.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260909001856/https://www.nationalgeographic.com/magazine/article/north-americas-most-valuable-resource-is-at-risk-feature|archivedate=9 September 2026}}
## {{lb|en|cooking}} To prepare ([[food]]) for [[cooking#Noun|cooking]] or [[eating#Noun|eating]], especially by [[season#Verb|seasoning]] it; specifically, to add a [[dressing#Noun|dressing]] or [[sauce#Noun|sauce]] (to food, especially a [[salad]]). {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Peele Old Wives Tale|lines=142–143|page=14|passage=''Old vvom''[''an'']: [O]nce vppon a time his daughter was ſtollen avvay, and hee ſent ſo long, that he ſent all his men out of his Land. / ''Frol''[''icke'']: VVho '''dreſt''' his dinner then?}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Taming of the Shrew|act=IV|scene=iii|page=223|column=2|passage=Heere Loue, thou ſeeſt how diligent I am, / To '''dreſſe''' thy meate my ſelfe, and bring it thee.}}
##* {{RQ:Milton Poems|poem=L'Allegro|page=34|passage=Where ''Corydon'' and ''Thyrſis'' met, / Are at their ſavory dinner ſet / Of Hearbes, and other Country Meſſes, / Which the neat-handed ''Phillis'' '''dreſſes'''; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Austen Pride and Prejudice|volume=II|chapter=XVI|page=188|passage=These two girls had been above an hour in the place, happily employed in visiting an opposite milliner, watching the sentinel on guard, and '''dressing''' a sallad and cucumber.}}
## {{lb|en|film|television|theater}}
### To design, make, or [[prepare]] [[costume#Noun|costumes]] (for a [[play#Noun|play]] or other [[performance]]); also, to [[present#Verb|present]] (a [[production]]) in a particular costume [[style#Noun|style]].
### To prepare (a [[set#Noun|set]]) by [[install]]ing the [[prop#Noun|props]], [[scenery]], etc.
###* {{quote-book|en|author=Marvin Silbersher|chapter=22|title=A Fistful of Stars|location=[Bloomington, Ind.]|publisher=w:Xlibris|year=2012|page=106|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=aVBRAAAAQBAJ&pg=PA106|isbn=978-1-4691-8127-1|passage=Mallory, all night long, single-handedly painted and '''dressed''' the set so that at eight o'clock Sunday morning when we arrived to make breakfast in the kitchen, there she was sound asleep on the davenport in the set, every prop in place.}}
## {{lb|en|military}} To arrange ([[soldier#Noun|soldiers]] or [[troop#Noun|troops]]) into [[proper#Adjective|proper]] [[formation]]; especially, to [[adjust]] (soldiers or troops) into [[straight line]]s and at a proper [[distance#Noun|distance]] from each other; to [[align]].
##: {{ux|en|to '''dress''' the ranks}}
## {{lb|en|Northern England|archaic}} To treat (someone) in a particular manner; specifically, in an [[appropriate]] or [[fitting#Adjective|fitting]] manner; {{lb|en|by extension|ironic}} to give (someone) a [[deserved#Adjective|deserved]] [[beating#Noun|beating]]; also, to give (someone) a [[good#Adjective|good]] [[scolding#Noun|scolding]]; to [[dress down]].
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Measure|I|i
|passage=[...] I say, bid come before vs Angelo:<br>What figure of vs thinke you, he will beare.<br>For you must know, we haue with speciall soule<br>Elected him our absence to supply;<br>Lent him our terror, '''drest''' him with our loue,<br>And giuen his Deputation all the Organs<br>Of our owne powre: What thinke you of it?}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[break in]] and [[train#Verb|train]] (a [[horse#Noun|horse]] or other animal) for use.
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 2 Q1|act=V|scene=v|page=78|passage=Oh how it ernd my hart when I beheld, / In London ſtreetes, that Coronation day, / VVhen Bullingbroke rode on Roane Barbarie. / That horſe, that thou ſo often haſt beſtride. / That horſe, that I ſo carefully haue '''dreſt'''.}}
