# {{lb|en|chiefly|US|Philippines}} A place where [[trains]] stop for passengers to embark and disembark.
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=The Great Gold Robbery. Extraordinary Disclosures.|journal=[[w:The Daily News (UK)|The Daily News]]|issue=3,275|location=London|date=14 November 1856|page=5|pageurl=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/390827140/|column=3|passage=I got into the cab and told the cabman to drive up to the Dover '''train station'''.}}

====Usage notes====
''Train station'' now dominates in American use, and it has increased in popularity in British writing since the 1990s, but is still less common in writing than ''railway station''. Just ''station'' is usually used in speech and is usually understood to mean one used by trains, especially for a known long distance fare.<ref>[https://books.google.com/ngrams/graph?content=train+station%2Crailway+station%2Crailroad+station&year_start=1900&year_end=2008&corpus=18&smoothing=3&share=&direct_url=t1%3B%2Ctrain%20station%3B%2Cc0%3B.t1%3B%2Crailway%20station%3B%2Cc0%3B.t1%3B%2Crailroad%20station%3B%2Cc0 "train station", "railway station", "railroad station"], Google Ngrams</ref>

''Train station'' is sometimes seen as an [[Americanism]] in British usage due to the term becoming popular there before Britain.<ref>2006 June 1, Peter Hitchens, "[http://hitchensblog.mailonsunday.co.uk/2006/06/do_you_speak_am.html Do you speak American?]", ''The Mail Online''</ref>

