near.content:
{{also|Near|ne-ar}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|en|non-rhotic}}
** {{IPA|en|/nɪə/|[nɪə̯]|a=RP}}, {{enPR|nîr)}}
*** {{audio|en|En-uk-near.ogg|a=London }}
*** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Back ache-near.wav|a=London}}
** {{IPA|en|/nɪː/|/nɪj.ə/|a=SSB}}
** {{IPA|en|/nɛː/|a=East Anglia,near-square}}
** {{enPR|nîr|a=AU}}; {{IPA|en|/nɪə̯/|[nɪː]}}
** {{enPR|nîr|a=NZ}}; {{IPA|en|/niə̯/}}
* {{a|en|rhotic}}
** {{enPR|nîr|a=GA,CA}}; {{IPA|en|/nɪɹ/}}
*** {{audio|en|en-us-near.ogg|a=California}}
** {{IPA|en|/niɾ/|[niːɾ]|a=Scotland}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪə(ɹ)|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|nere}}, {{m|enm|ner|}}, from {{inh|en|ang|nēar|t=nearer|pos=comparative of {{m|ang|nēah|t=nigh}}, the superlative would become {{m|en|next}}}}, influenced by {{der|en|non|nær|t=near}}, both originating from {{der|en|gem-pro|*nēhwiz|t=nearer}}, comparative of the adverb {{m|gem-pro|*nēhw|t=near}}, from the adjective {{m|gem-pro|*nēhwaz}}, ultimately from Pre-Proto-Germanic {{m|ine-pro||*h₂nḗḱwos}}, a lengthened-grade adjective derived from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₂neḱ-|t=to reach}}.
{{col-top|2|cog}}
Cognate with {{cog|frr|nai}}, {{m|frr|noi}}, {{m|frr|näi|t=close, near}}, {{cog|stq|nai|t=close, near}}, {{cog|nl|na|t=close}}, {{m|nl|naar|t=to, towards}}, {{cog|nds-nl|nao|t=after}}, {{cog|de|nach|t=after}}, {{m|de|nahe|t=near}}, {{m|de|näher|t=nearer}}, {{cog|nds-de|nao}}, {{m|nds-de|nå|t=towards}}, {{cog|lb|no|t=after}}, {{cog|da,fo,is,nb,nn|nær|t=close, near}}, {{cog|sv|när}}, {{m|sv|nära|t=close, near}}, {{cog|got|𐌽𐌴𐍈|t=close, near}}. See also {{m|en|nigh}}.

''Near'' appears to be derived from (or at the very least influenced by) the North Germanic languages; as opposed to {{m|en|nigh}}, which continues the inherited West Germanic adjective. Both, however, are ultimately derived from the same Proto-Germanic root: {{m|gem-pro|*nēhw|t=near, close}}.
{{col-bottom}}

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|er}}

# Physically [[close]].
#: {{syn|en|close}}
#: {{ant|en|remote}}
#: {{ux|en|I can't see '''near''' objects very clearly without my glasses.}}
#: {{ux|en|Stay '''near''' at all times.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1914|author=w:Irvin S. Cobb|title=Europe Revised
|passage=At the end of the line '''nearest''' the Arch, under a flary light, stood an old bearded man having the look on his face of a kindly but somewhat irritated moo-cow.}}
#* {{RQ:Bramah Eyes of Max Carrados|passage=Without any ceremony the '''near''' man [[let drive]] at my throat with one of those beastly wavy daggers they go in for.}}
# Close in time.
#: {{ux|en|The end is '''near'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|We're getting '''near''' to Christmas.}}
# Closely connected or related.
#: {{ux|en|The deceased man had no '''near''' relatives.}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Leviticus|18|12|passage=she is thy fathers '''neere''' kinswoman.}}
# Close to one's interests, affection, etc.; intimate; dear.
#: {{ux|en|It is a matter of '''near''' consequence to me.}}
#: {{ux|en|a '''near''' friend}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2017|author=Velvel Pasternak|title=Behind the Music, Stories, Anecdotes, Articles and Reflections|page=225|text=The ''Besht'' preached that the simple man, imbued with native faith and able to pray fervently and wholeheartedly with a sense of joy in his heart, was '''nearer''' and dearer to God than the learned but joyless formalist spending his whole life in the study of Talmud.}}
# Close to anything followed or imitated; not free, loose, or rambling.
#: {{ux|en|a version '''near''' to the original}}
# So as barely to avoid or pass injury or loss; close; narrow.
#: {{ux|en|a '''near''' escape}}
# [[approximate|Approximate]], [[almost]].
#: {{ux|en|The two words are '''near''' synonyms.}}
#: {{ux|en|living in '''near''' poverty}}
# {{lb|en|British|in relation to a vehicle}} On the side nearest to the kerb (the left-hand side if one drives on the left).
#: {{ant|en|off}}
#: {{ux|en|The '''near''' front wheel came loose.}}
# {{lb|en|dated}} Next to the driver, when he is on foot; {{lb|en|US}} on the left of an animal or a team.
#: {{ux|en|the '''near''' ox; the '''near''' leg}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Immediate; direct; close; short.
#* {{RQ:Milton Poems 1673|sonnet=XVII|page=61|text=Toward ſolid good what leads the '''neareſt''' way;}}
# {{lb|en|now|rare}} [[stingy|Stingy]]; [[parsimonious]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|Don't be '''near''' with your pocketbook.}}
#* {{RQ:Burney Cecilia|volume=II|chapter=I|passage=[T]o let you know, Miss, he's so '''near''', it's partly a wonder how he lives at all: and yet he's worth a power of money, too.}}
# {{lb|en|programming|not comparable}} Within the currently selected [[segment]] in a segmented [[memory]] [[architecture]].
#: {{antonyms|en|far}}
#: {{ux|en|a '''near''' pointer}}

=====Usage notes=====
* {{m|en|near|Near}} was originally the [[comparative]] form of {{m|en|nigh}}; the [[superlative]] form was {{m|en|next}}. {{m|en||Nigh}} is used today mostly in archaic, poetic, or regional contexts.

