# At or towards a position close in space or time. 
#: {{ux|en|We are getting '''near''' to Christmas.}}
#: {{ux|en|He took a step '''nearer''' to the water.}}
<!-- despite other dictionaries giving examples such as "draw near", "come near", etc., I feel unhappy that "near" in these cases is truly adverbial. It seems more like an adjective complement. Note also that "stay near" is (at the time of writing) given above as an adjectival example. -->
# [[nearly|Nearly]]; [[almost]].
#: {{ux|en|He was '''near''' unconscious when I found him.}}
#: {{ux|en|I jumped into the '''near'''-freezing water.}}
#: {{ux|en|I '''near''' ruptured myself trying to move the piano.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1666|author=w:Samuel Pepys|title=Diary and Correspondence|year_published=1867
|passage={{...}} he hears for certain that the Queen-Mother is about and hath '''near''' finished a peace with France {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1825|author=w:David Hume; w:Tobias George Smollett|title=The History of England|page=263
|passage=Sir John Friend had very '''near''' completed a regiment of horse.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1886|translator=H.L. Br&aelig;kstad|author=w:Peter Christen Asbj&oslash;rnsen|title=Folk and Fairy Tales|page=169|passage=Peter ran after them as fast as his legs would carry him, but at last he had only one of the hares left, and when this was gone, he was very '''near burst''' with running.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2003|author=Owen Parry|title=Honor's Kingdom|page=365
|passage=Thinking about those pounds and pence, I '''near''' forgot my wound.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|author=w:Jimmy Buffett|title=A Salty Piece of Land|page=315
|passage="I damn '''near''' forgot." He pulled an envelope from his jacket.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=Juliet Marillier|title=The Dark Mirror|page=377
|passage=The fire was almost dead, the chamber '''near''' dark.}}

=====Usage notes=====
The sense of ''nearly'' or ''almost'' is dialect, colloquial, old-fashioned or poetic in certain uses, such as, in many cases, when ''near'' is used to directly modify a verb.

