magic.content:
{{also|Magic|magič|màgic|MAGIC}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|magick||fantasy|or|occult|otherwise|obsolete}}
* {{alt|en|magicke|magique||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
{{ety|en|:inh|enm:magik<id:magical>|enm:magik<id:magic>|tree=1}}

From {{inh|en|enm|magik,magyk}}, from {{der|en|fro|magique|pos=noun and adjective}}, from {{der|en|la|magicus|pos=a}}, {{m|la|magica|pos=noun use of feminine form of {{m|la|magicus}}}}, from {{der|en|grc|μαγικός||magical}}, from {{m|grc|μάγος||[[magician]], [[magus]]}}. Ultimately from {{uder|en|ira-old|-}}, probably derived from {{der|en|ine-pro|*megʰ-|*meh₂gʰ-|to be able to, to help; power, sorcerer}}. {{surf|en|mage|-ic}}. {{piecewise doublet|en|magey}}. {{displaced|ang|ġealdor}} (whence {{ncog|enm|galder}}) and {{m|en|dwimmer}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈmæd͡ʒɪk/|a=RP,GA}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-magic.ogg|a=GA}}
* {{rhymes|en|ædʒɪk|s=2}}
* {{hyphenation|en|mag|ic}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# The application of [[ritual]]s or actions, especially those based on [[occult]] knowledge, to [[subdue]] or [[manipulate]] natural or [[supernatural]] beings and [[force]]s in order to have some benefit from them. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#: {{syn|en|dwimmer|spellcraft|thaumaturgy|witchcraft|wizardry|Thesaurus:magic}}
#: {{uxi|en|“My '''magic''' will help you find true love,” said the witch as she looked for the right herbs in her collection.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=c. 1489|publisher=w:William Caxton|title=Foure Sonnes of Aymon
|passage=And whan he shall be arrayed as I telle you / lete hym thenne doo his incantacyons & his '''magyke''' as he wyll […].}}
#* {{RQ:Basse Pastorals|poem=The Metamorphosis of the Wallnut-tree of Borestall. In an Eglogue and 3 Cantos, betweene Jasper and Jefferye.|canto=2|stanza=19|page=122|passage=But by what '''magique''' I, that here have ſtood / Four hunderd yeares (thou know’ſt how truly ſpoke), / Can now remove, think’ſt thou?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1781|author=w:Edward Gibbon|title=Decline and Fall of the Roman Empire|section=II.23
|passage=The arts of '''magic''' and divination were strictly prohibited.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1864|author={{w|Henry William Dulcken}}|title=Dalziel's Illustrated Arabian Nights' Entertainments|location=London|publisher=Ward, Lock, and Tyler|volume=|page=584|passage="There is no doubt that this African Magician was not the brother of Mustapha the tailor, as he had pretented to be, and consequently not the uncle of Aladdin. He was most probably a native of Africa, as that is a country where '''magic''' is more studied than in any other."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1928|author=w:Lewis Spence|title=Mysteries of Britain|page=viii. 192|passage=Does not the very name stir the heart [...] with a thrill more mysterious and romantic than any allusion to the '''magics''' of Egypt or Hind?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1971|author={{w|Keith Thomas (historian)|Keith Thomas}}|title=Religion and the Decline of Magic|page=23|publisher=Folio Society|year_published=2012
|passage=Conversions to the new religion {{...}} have frequently been assisted by the view of converts that they are acquiring not just a means of otherworldly salvation, but a new and more powerful '''magic'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1973|author={{w|Arthur C. Clark}}|title=Profiles of the future|edition=revised|passage=Any sufficiently advanced technology is indistinguishable from '''magic'''. }}
## The supernatural forces which are drawn on in such a ritual.
## {{lb|en|rare|countable}} A specific [[ritual]] or procedure associated with such magic; a [[spell]]; a magical ability. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##: {{syn|en|cantrip|incantation|Thesaurus:magic spell}}
##* {{RQ:C. S. Lewis Lion|passage=And she can turn people into stone and do all kinds of horrible things. And she has made a '''magic''' so that it is always winter in Narnia—always winter, but it never gets to Christmas.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2017|author=Neil Gaiman|authorlink=Neil Gaiman|publisher=Bloomsbury Publishing|title=Norse Mythology|page=116|passage="I am the master of mighty '''magics'''."}}
## {{lb|en|gaming|countable}} The ability to cast a magic spell.
##: {{usex|en|I learned a new '''magic''' after rescuing the tree fairy.}}
# Something producing successful and remarkable results, especially when not fully understood; an [[enchanting]] quality; exceptional skill. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=The Elopers|page=25|passage=The original family who had begun to build a palace to outrival Nonesuch had died out before they had put up little more than the gateway, so that the actual structure which had come down to posterity retained the secret '''magic''' of a promise rather than the overpowering splendour of a great architectural achievement.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Diary|author={{w|Lou Sullivan}}|date=10 Feb 1965|text=I look at him and there is a '''magic'''. What is it? Love?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1969|author=w:Patsy Adam-Smith|title=Folklore of the Australian Railwaymen|publisher=Macmillan|location=Melbourne|page=151|passage=We began to take Santa Claus to the kids at the isolated sidings for the same reason men all over Australia on the lines back o' beyond did [...] we didn't want them to miss that '''magic'''.}}
## {{lb|en|computing|slang}} [[complicated|Complicated]] or [[esoteric]] [[code]] that is not expected to be generally understood.
