street.content:
{{also|Street}}
==English==
{{wp}}
[[File:Tverskaya Street2.jpg|thumb|A radial '''street''' in [[Moscow]], Russia]]

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|streete|streat|streate||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
{{ety|en|:inh|enm:strete|tree=1}}

{{inh+|en|enm|strete}}, from {{inh|en|ang-ang|strēt||street|ng=compare {{cog|ang-wsx|strǣt}}}} from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*strātu|t=street}}, an early {{lg|borrowing}} from {{der|en|la-lat|([[via]]) [[strāta]]|t=paved (road)}}, from {{der|en|la|strātus}}, past participle of {{m|la|sternō||stretch out, spread, bestrew with, cover, pave}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*sterh₃-||to stretch out, extend, spread}}. {{dbt|en|estrade|stratum}}.

The {{IPAchar|/aː/}} vowel of the Latin etymon shifted by {{w|Anglo-Frisian brightening}} to {{IPAchar|/æː/}} in West Saxon and {{IPAchar|/eː/}} in Anglian Old English; these developed respectively to {{IPAchar|/ɛː/}} and {{IPAchar|/eː/}} in Middle English, {{IPAchar|/ɛː/}} and {{IPAchar|/iː/}}  in Early Modern English, and finally {{IPAchar|/iː/}} in Modern English by the {{w|Great Vowel Shift}}. The modern spelling reflects the Anglian form, as in {{m|en|sleep}}, {{m|en|greedy}}, {{m|en|sheep}}.

{{col-top|2|cog}}
Cognate with {{cog|sco|stret}}, {{m|sco|strete}}, {{m|sco|streit||street}}, {{cog|frr|Straat}}, {{m|frr|stroot}}, {{m|frr|struat|t=street}} (North Frisian forms are borrowed from {{cog|gml|strâte}}), {{cog|stq|Sträite|t=street}}, {{cog|fy|strjitte|t=street}}, {{cog|bar|Stråßn|t=street}}, {{cog|nl|straat|t=street}} (see doublet {{dbt|en|straat|notext=1}}), {{cog|de|Strasse}}, {{m|de|Straße|t=street}}, {{cog|nds-de|Straat}}, {{m|nds|Straote|t=street}}, {{cog|li|sjtraot}}, {{m|li|straot|t=street}}, {{cog|lb|Strooss|t=street}}, {{cog|mhn|stros|t=street}}, {{cog|wym|śtrös}}, {{m|wym|štrȫs|t=street}}, {{cog|yi|שטראָז|t=street}}, {{cog|da|stræde|t=alley, lane, narrow street}}, {{cog|fo,is|stræti|t=street}}, {{cog|nb|strede|t=narrow street}}, {{cog|sv|stråt|t=path, road, route; way, course}} (Scandinavian forms are borrowed from Old English), {{cog|pt,es|estrada|t=road}}, {{cog|it|strada|t=road, street}}.

Related to {{cog|ang|strēowian}}, {{m|ang|strewian||to strew, scatter}}, {{cog|la|sternō}}, {{cog|grc|στορνύναι}}. See more at {{l|en|strew}}.
{{col-bottom}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|strēt}}
** {{IPA|en|/stɹiːt/|[ʃt͡ʃɹiːt]<a:/str/-retraction>|a=RP}}
** {{a|en|US}}
*** {{IPA|en|/stɹit/|a=GA}}
*** {{IPA|en|[ʃtɹ̠̊iʔ]|a=Philadelphia}}
*** {{IPA|en|[skɹitˀ]|[ʃkɹitˀ]|a=AAE}}
* {{audio|en|En-uk-a street.ogg|a=RP}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Xavier hillier-street.wav|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-street.ogg|a=GA}}
* {{rhymes|en|iːt|s=1}}
* {{hyph|en|street}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[paved]] part of [[road]], usually in a [[village]] or a [[town]].
#: {{ux|en|Walk down the '''street''' until you see a hotel on the right.}}
#: {{ux|en| The man wearing a red coat is standing on the '''street'''.}}
# A road as above, but including the [[sidewalk]]s ([[pavement]]s) and [[building]]s.
#: {{syn|en|way}}
#: {{ux|en|I live on the '''street''' down from Joyce Avenue.}}
#: {{ux|en|I've been shopping in Oxford '''Street'''.}}
# {{lb|en|specifically|US|Canada}} The roads that run perpendicular to [[avenue]]s in a [[grid]] layout.
# {{n-g|{{lg|metonym|Metonymic}} senses:}}
## The [[people]] who live in such a road, as a [[neighborhood]].
## The people who spend a great deal of time on the street in [[urban]] areas, especially, the [[young]], the [[poor]], the [[unemployed]], and those engaged in [[illegal]] activities.
##* {{RQ:Noire Thug-A-Licious|page=24|passage=Take or be taken. Get yours or get got. It was the code of the '''streets''' and I'd lived by it. The way things was looking, I was prolly gone die by it too.}}
## An illicit or contraband [[source]], especially of [[drug]]s.
##: {{ux|en|I got some pot cheap on the '''street'''.}}
##: {{ux|en|The seized drugs had a [[street value|'''street''' value]] of $5 million.}}
